{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "def largest_prime_factor(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n. Assumes n > 1.\"\"\"\n    largest = None\n\n    # Handle factor 2 separately by dividing n by 2 until odd.\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # For odd potential divisors d starting at 3, increment by 2.\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        if n % d == 0:\n            largest = d\n            # Divide out all occurrences of d.\n            while n % d == 0:\n                n //= d\n        d += 2\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be the largest prime factor.\n    if n > 1:\n        largest = n\n\n    return largest"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/60", "solution": "def sum_to_n(n: int):\n    \"\"\"sum_to_n is a function that sums numbers from 1 to n.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> sum_to_n(30)\n    465\n    >>> sum_to_n(100)\n    5050\n    >>> sum_to_n(5)\n    15\n    >>> sum_to_n(10)\n    55\n    >>> sum_to_n(1)\n    1\n\n    Parameters:\n    n (int): The upper bound of the sum (inclusive). Must be an integer >= 1.\n\n    Returns:\n    int: The sum of integers from 1 to n.\n\n    Raises:\n    TypeError: If n is not an int.\n    ValueError: If n < 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be >= 1\")\n    return n * (n + 1) // 2"}
