{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a list of length n representing a pile\n    of stones. The first level has n stones. Each subsequent level has the\n    next odd number if the current is odd, or the next even number if the\n    current is even (equivalently, add 2 each level).\n\n    Example:\n    >>> make_a_pile(3)\n    [3, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer\")\n\n    return [n + 2 * i for i in range(n)]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, you have to make a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones.\n    The number of stones in the next level is:\n        - the next odd number if n is odd.\n        - the next even number if n is even.\n    Return the number of stones in each level in a list, where element at index\n    i represents the number of stones in the level (i+1).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer\")\n\n    # Each subsequent level increases by 2, preserving parity of the starting n.\n    return [n + 2 * i for i in range(n)]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import decimal\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Returns the closest integer to a string representation of a number,\n    rounding away from zero for equidistant values.\n    '''\n    # Create a Decimal from the stripped string to handle various formats and ensure precision.\n    d_value = decimal.Decimal(value.strip())\n    # Quantize to the nearest integer (represented by Decimal('1')) using ROUND_HALF_UP.\n    # ROUND_HALF_UP rounds .5 values away from zero, satisfying the tie-breaking rule.\n    rounded_decimal = d_value.quantize(decimal.Decimal('1'), rounding=decimal.ROUND_HALF_UP)\n    # Convert the resulting Decimal to an integer and return it.\n    return int(rounded_decimal)"}
